WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.080
Please give a big applause for Jan.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.200
Jan Koum grew up in a rural village outside of&nbsp;
Kyiv, Ukraine when it was still part of the Soviet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:15.200
Union. It was a tough time to be Jewish so the 16-year-old and his mom decided to move to America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.640
They packed suitcases with notebooks so they&nbsp;
wouldn't have to pay for supplies when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.680
arrived in Silicon Valley. His father was supposed&nbsp;
to join them but never made it. He fell ill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:29.040
and died a few years later. Koum swept the floor&nbsp;
of a grocery store to make ends meet. His mom also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.360
got a job but then she got cancer. They lived off&nbsp;
food stamps and her disability payments. She passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.800
away three years after his dad died. Such a start&nbsp;
in life would be difficult for anyone to overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.720
yet overcome he did. As a teen, he taught himself&nbsp;
about computer networking by reading books from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.000
used bookstore and returned them to get his money&nbsp;
back. He enrolled at San Jose State University but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.800
dropped out when Yahoo! offered him a job as an&nbsp;
infrastructure engineer. That's also where Brian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.840
Acton worked. The two got along really well and&nbsp;
after almost a decade at Yahoo!, they quit took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.760
a year off to travel all over South America and&nbsp;
then applied for jobs at Facebook but got rejected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.000
That didn't deter Koum. He had enough money&nbsp;
saved from his time at Yahoo! to launch his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.520
venture alongside Acton - a communications&nbsp;
app called WhatsApp. The app exploded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.080
popularity and ironically would go on&nbsp;
to be bought by Facebook for $19 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.600
Time and time again, immigrants appear to&nbsp;
rise to the top despite the roadblocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.280
Why is that? There's a concept called burning&nbsp;
the boats before getting to shore made famous by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:40.880
Spanish conquistador Hernán Cortés. In 1519, he led&nbsp;
an expedition of 600 Spaniards aboard 11 ships to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:47.520
Mexico to explore and secure it for colonization.&nbsp;
Upon arriving, Cortés decided to destroy the fleet -&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.240
a way of signaling to his men that there was&nbsp;
no turning back. They would either succeed or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:58.000
perish. Within two years, Cortés conquered the&nbsp;
mighty Aztec Empire. It seems the burning the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.120
boats analogy can be applied to immigrants. They're&nbsp;
driven to succeed because there's no turning back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.960
And what leads to their billion dollar idea&nbsp;
could very well be the fact that they grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.920
up in another country. Researchers who studied&nbsp;
their success note cross-cultural experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.040
increases the ability to recognize entrepreneurial&nbsp;
opportunities. Koum would have never come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.480
the idea for WhatsApp had he not grown up&nbsp;
in the USSR. I remember my parents not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.280
able to have a conversation on the phone. The&nbsp;
walls had ears and you couldn't speak freely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.520
Being shaped by those experiences, it's extremely&nbsp;
important to make sure that we provide the level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.560
of privacy and security that people would be able&nbsp;
to use our product freely and not being afraid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.680
His is one of many immigrant success stories.&nbsp;
More than half of the top Fortune 500 companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.320
were founded by immigrants or the child of an&nbsp;
immigrant. Without immigrants, there might very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.240
well be no Google. When co-founder Sergey Brin was&nbsp;
six years old, his family fled anti-semitism in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.600
former Soviet Union and escaped to America. While&nbsp;
studying computer science at Stanford, Brin met&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.080
fellow student Larry Page. The two developed&nbsp;
the Google search engine which they called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.840
Backrub. They almost sold it to the web portal&nbsp;
Excite for a mere $750,000 but luckily for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.360
co-founders, the deal never went through because&nbsp;
Google is now worth over a trillion dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.680
When Page and Brin stepped back from Google in&nbsp;
its parent company Alphabet, they handed the reins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.720
to Sundar Pichai, an immigrant from India. He was&nbsp;
a bright student in his native country, getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.080
himself into the prestigious Indian Institute of&nbsp;
Technology Kharagpur, where he studied metallurgical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.560
engineering. The US then welcomed Pichai with&nbsp;
scholarships to pursue a Master's degree at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.200
Stanford and then an MBA at Wharton. Another&nbsp;
reason researchers attribute to the success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.480
of Pichai and others like him is that immigration&nbsp;
policies in many economically advanced countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.400
favor highly skilled individuals, including those&nbsp;
with a college degree. Just 11 years after joining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.000
Google, he rose to become CEO. His drive to build&nbsp;
and innovate convinced the founders to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.960
their own browser, Google Chrome. He once told an&nbsp;
audience that the following words by American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.640
inventor Thomas Edison sums up what it means to be&nbsp;
an entrepreneur: I have not failed, I've just found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.240
10,000 ways that don't work. That never give&nbsp;
up attitude is also at the center of Eric Yuan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:16.240
success. He's not a household name but the company&nbsp;
he created is being used in nearly every household.&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.400
Many of us have been doing our jobs from&nbsp;
home during the pandemic thanks to Zoom.&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.840
Yuan was inspired by a talk Microsoft co-founder Bill&nbsp;
Gates gave in Asia in the nineties about the potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.960
of the internet. Yuan wanted in on the dot com boom&nbsp;
and knew he had to leave China and go to America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:37.120
to do it. The problem was the US government denied&nbsp;
his visa application not once not twice not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.320
three times. He didn't get the green light&nbsp;- or green card in this case - until his ninth&nbsp;try. 

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.360
He barely spoke English when he arrived&nbsp;
but he did know the language of the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.240
He could code, landing an engineering job with&nbsp;
Webex - a video calling software company later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.280
bought by Cisco. His salary was reportedly&nbsp;
in the high six figures but he wasn't happy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:02.400
He knew the technology could be better so he&nbsp;
left to form his own company. Today, Yuan is number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:09.040
43 on the Forbes list of the 400 wealthiest people&nbsp;
in America. There also happens to be 43 immigrants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:15.440
on this list. Holding the top spot is Amazon's CEO&nbsp;
Jeff Bezos. Bezos may have been born in the US but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.360
his father is Miguel Bezos - a refugee who's not his&nbsp;
biological dad but raised him as his own. He fled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.920
the Castro regime in Cuba when he was 16 years&nbsp;
old. He was all by himself. I have a hard time even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.800
imagining that. He was a member of Operation&nbsp;
Peter Pan, a rescue program for children run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.920
by the Catholic church and heavily funded by the&nbsp;
American government. Miguel's mother thought the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.280
would be really cold compared to tropical Cuba so&nbsp;
she made him a heavy jacket from cleaning cloths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.960
the only material she could get her hands on. It's&nbsp;
now framed in his house. When he landed in America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:54.400
he couldn't speak any English but figured it out.&nbsp;
He got a scholarship to a university in New Mexico&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.000
and also took a job at a bank for extra cash where&nbsp;
he met Jeff's mom Jackie. Jeff Bezos has spoken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.680
proudly of his father and how much he inspired&nbsp;
him because of his determination, optimism, and grit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.880
That mindset helped the young Bezos guide Amazon&nbsp;
through the turbulent dot-com bubble when many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.920
tech companies folded, and led them to take risks -&nbsp;
like offering free shipping to entice customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:21.040
even though the math didn't make sense. Such&nbsp;
strategies helped grow Amazon into the behemoth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:26.400
it is today. Psychologist Angela Duckworth is well&nbsp;
known for research on the factors that predict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.920
success. She says the key to success - the single&nbsp;
most important factor - is how gritty you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:38.160
writing in her book on the topic: Enthusiasm is&nbsp;
common, endurance is rare. Someone who knows a thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.600
or two about enduring is Elon Musk who emigrated&nbsp;
from South Africa to the US by way of Canada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:48.880
where he spent a couple of years as an undergrad.&nbsp;
When asked by CBS' 60 minutes whether he considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:55.200
packing it in after SpaceX's third launch failed,&nbsp;
he responded: I never give up. I'd have to be dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:01.840
or completely incapacitated. Thankfully he didn't&nbsp;
give up because the fourth flight was a success.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.720
One day, that grit may see Musk arriving on&nbsp;
the doorstep of Mars. Many who venture to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.520
new country are resilient but it's certainly&nbsp;
not the only factor that determines success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.080
They also have the technical knowledge to back&nbsp;
up their ambitions. A problem-solving website and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:24.880
app called Brilliant can help put you on the&nbsp;
path to success and it's free to sign up. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.600
been workig my way through the logic course which&nbsp;
provides a solid foundation for their math courses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.560
Whether you like to brush up on calculus, practice&nbsp;
programming, or learn the fundamentals of computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:39.200
science, Brilliant is great for both beginners&nbsp;
and those looking for a refresher. There are no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:44.160
tests and if you're stuck on a problem, Brilliant&nbsp;
explains how to get the right answer. To sign up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.760
head to brilliant.org/newsthink. The link is in&nbsp;
my description And the first 200 people to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:54.960
my link will get 20% off the Premium subscription&nbsp;
which allows you to access all courses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.920
Thanks for watching Newsthink. I'm Cindy Pom.

